# Pinning for the qemu-pmb887x wasm build.
#
# The qemu source is the root-level qemu/ submodule (Azq2/qemu-pmb887x,
# local branch wasm-browser-port = origin/wasm-patches): the whole
# wasm/TCI/perf series committed on top of qemu-pmb887x master
# (8b9d485bc2), plus the AFE fix cherry-picked from alula's dsp-stuff
# branch and the per-board RTC-seed commit. (The former patches/*.patch
# files were removed on 2026-09-12; the commits are the series.)
# The pmb887x-emu meta-repo is checked out alongside at master; its own
# qemu submodule is not used (the root qemu/ submodule is the build
# source), but the repo is where the page's Siemens module comes from:
# the siemensfw library (src/siemens) that scripts/build-recalc-wasm.sh
# compiles -- key recalculation, ESN recovery and the Siemens half of
# device detection (probeFullflash).
#
# Why not plain master: master alone aborts every Siemens fullflash
# during L1 GSM frame handling (">>EXIT<< FILE: l1bbcsg / CepName:
# H_GSMFR" at ~25-30 s — the L1<->DSP handshake starves); the "hacky AFE
# (LLE+HLE)" commit from dsp-stuff fixes s75/el71/c81.
#   qemu 4e60f930b2   (2026-09-22 round forty-seven: mrs cpsr emitted inline on
#                    wasm64 -- SL65 video -2.5 % ms/Mi)
#   qemu 520d682b91   (2026-09-22 round forty-seven: comment only -- the
#                    arm_rebuild_hflags price is 12.3 ns, not 31.6)
#   qemu 18f8cd44e9   (2026-09-22 round forty-seven: a timer re-armed on the
#                    main-loop thread no longer notifies the main loop --
#                    KE970 boot mlWake -45 %, milestones 3.5-4.9 % sooner)
#   qemu 46d7aa3e64   (2026-09-22 round forty-seven: the exception return
#                    stops taking the BQL around empty hook lists but keeps
#                    releasing a deferred hold -- video -5.7 % ms/Mi; keeping
#                    the hold across the return deadlocked KE970's idle spin)
#   qemu c0a93d01a2   (2026-09-22 round forty-seven: lockstep budget exit()
#                    gives back a deferred BQL first -- the page froze on it)
#   qemu 4457396bbf   (2026-09-22 round forty-seven: flash write-behind goes
#                    asynchronous -- the synchronous flush polled the
#                    thread pool with the BQL held, ~240 ms of every KE970
#                    boot second, and the vCPU's flash MMIO waited it out.
#                    KE970 flash phase 30 -> 25.7 s, 1.6 G insns 41 -> 37 s)
#   qemu 95055ff451   (2026-09-22 round forty-seven: 16 MB phys-summary
#                    buckets -- a KE970 romd flip walked the running code
#                    in the other half of its 32 MB bucket, ~2 300 entries
#                    per flip; now 68.  ~4 % of the vCPU through the LG
#                    flash-programming boot, C-side timer)
#   qemu 1a043d7c57   (2026-09-22 round forty-seven: flash-blk schedules
#                    its write-behind flush only when the dirty list
#                    starts -- a pending BH still aio_notify()s, so it was
#                    a main-loop futex wake per programmed word, ~140k/s
#                    through a KE970 boot.  mlWake -75..-83 %)
#   qemu 6e361b6c8e   (2026-09-22 round forty-seven: capcom times only the
#                    events that can raise an interrupt.  02ada89fe6 armed
#                    a timer per wrap and compare; the SL65's T0 PWM made
#                    that ~360 k icount exits per virtual second and cut
#                    video rt 2.5 -> 0.38 through rounds 45-46.  Back to
#                    rt 2.40, execIter 34/Mi)
#   qemu 275f61c     (2026-09-16 round twenty-five, 0101-0103: the
#                    interpreter tier, and the two things it retired.
#                    0101: the backend records every emitter's already
#                    register-allocated operands into a flat uint32 op
#                    stream (tcg/wasm64/w64-interp.h) and a C interpreter
#                    in the main module runs a TB before its module
#                    exists, so a batch is no longer closed early by the
#                    first member that has to run.  0102: speculative
#                    successor translation defaults OFF under the tier --
#                    round 22 priced it at a 12.5 % break-even because a
#                    miss forced a module, and a miss no longer does, so
#                    break-even moves to ~100 % against a 66 % conversion.
#                    0103: W64_BATCH_N 128 -> 256, a cap that could not
#                    bind until batches filled.  EL71 12 s windows:
#                    347 -> 576.7 Mi, +66.2 %; modules 18 650 -> 698,
#                    module time 2.02 -> 0.34 s, members per module
#                    5.4 -> 152.  Two invariants broke outside the
#                    backend and both failed silently: 32-bit signed
#                    compares were evaluated at 64 bits on zero-extended
#                    operands, so gen_tb_start's `count - n < 0` icount
#                    test never fired; and tb_add_jump must refuse to
#                    link a chain into a target that has no module yet,
#                    before the cmpxchg that claims jmp_dest[n].  Module
#                    time is now dominated by its per-member term, which
#                    inverts round 24's law and retires the close count
#                    as a lever; translation is now the biggest single
#                    item at ~17 % of wall)
#   qemu 313e769      (2026-09-16 round twenty-four, 0100: closePreEnt /
#                    closePreTb -- at each batch close, sum what the
#                    members have already executed (W64_TBHIST counts,
#                    exact per TB).  With W64_NOCLOSEEXEC=1, which defers
#                    the close so batches fill to W64_BATCH_N, that is
#                    the interpreter tier's COST measured on the real
#                    thing instead of assumed: closes 35 873 -> 1 353,
#                    module time 3.54 -> 0.548 s, and 7 001 592 entries
#                    (1.45 % of all) run before their batch closes.  At
#                    109 ns each that is 0.76 s spent to save 2.99 s --
#                    net +2.2 s of a ~27 s boot, ~8 %.  The same sweep
#                    fits the module cost law over a 48x range of members
#                    (W64_SPEC_N 4/32/64, W64_BATCH_N 16/32/128, deferred
#                    close): ~86 us per close + ~2.8 us per member, so
#                    96 % of module time is the fixed cost of closing too
#                    often and the close count is the only lever left.
#                    tools/modshape-probe.mjs finds no cheap corner in
#                    new WebAssembly.Module and withdraws round 19's
#                    "four fifths is cold cache" -- that measurement
#                    recompiled identical wire bytes, which V8 serves
#                    from a module cache; distinct bytes cost 2.0-3.8x
#                    more.  Instrumentation only)
#   qemu 6a11e50      (2026-09-16 round twenty-three, 0099: W64_TBHIST --
#                    a per-TB entry counter in the prologue (one i32
#                    load/add/store at a translation-time-constant
#                    address), which is the number open item 1 had been
#                    gated on since round twenty.  EL71: 27.2 % of
#                    translated TBs are NEVER entered; of those that are,
#                    35.9 % run exactly once and 74.0 % run <= 31 times,
#                    while 0.08 % of TBs take half of all 586 M entries.
#                    Bimodal with an enormous gap, and S75 agrees, so it
#                    is guest code not a board.  With tools/interp-probe.c
#                    -- the same interpreter loop compiles to wasm at
#                    NATIVE speed, 7.26-7.42 vs 7.30-7.43 ns/op, while
#                    emitted TB code runs 2.7x slower than native -- the
#                    interpreter tier prices at +1.8 s of a ~27 s boot
#                    (~6 %), range +0.25..+2.35 s.  Instrumentation only)
#   qemu 806bef8      (2026-09-16 round twenty-three, 0098: price the inline
#                    TLB probe, and the dispatch it feeds.  W64_TLBDUP
#                    emits N extra REAL probes per memop, each result to
#                    its own per-site slot: the probe is 5.07 % of EL71
#                    wall, a two-load replacement 2.33 %, a one-load v128
#                    one 4.09 % -- its cost is not its load count.  Sites
#                    stay on one page 94.7 % of the time (W64_TLBHIT).
#                    Built on those three numbers, a per-site cache in
#                    FRONT of the probe measured 4.4-5.0 % SLOWER: a
#                    duplicate-probe instrument emits straight-line code,
#                    so it prices DELETING that code, never inserting a
#                    branch ahead of it.  W64_XCOUNT counts TB exits:
#                    goto_tb which=0 41.4 %, which=1 29.8 %, goto_ptr
#                    28.8 %, self-chaining 4.8 %; 4.16 guest insns per TB
#                    entry, dispatch 9.1 % of wall.  Instrumentation only)
#   qemu d52696f      (2026-09-16 round twenty-two, 0097: price the miss
#                    stream and close "speculate harder".  39562 misses in
#                    3878 pages = 10.2 per page, and a page holds ~529
#                    basic blocks, so eager page translation is 7x
#                    negative.  W64_MISSDUMP: 31397 misses at 31397
#                    DISTINCT pcs (zero repeats), of which only **10.2 %**
#                    appear anywhere in the 64 MB image as a pointer --
#                    89.8 % are reached by computed addresses no scan can
#                    see.  Ceiling on every static-edge idea: 1.2 % of
#                    wall.  The 12.5 % pipeline needs the interpreter
#                    tier or nothing.  Instrumentation only)
#   qemu 8003584      (2026-09-16 round twenty-two, 0096: W64_NOPCREL --
#                    CF_PCREL is set on every ARM system-mode TB and
#                    nobody had asked what it costs.  Off: -0.7 % 2/3
#                    (noise) and +2 % misses, and tbGen does not move, so
#                    this firmware maps its code once and the generality
#                    is unused but not worth removing.  No saving because
#                    cpu_R[15] is a TCG global living in a wasm local --
#                    do not price a global access as a load on this
#                    backend.  Ships on, knob off, native untouched)
#   qemu b6fdf43      (2026-09-16 round twenty-two, 0095: W64_LINSPEC, a
#                    rejected speculation edge kept as its reproducer.
#                    Speculation already converts at 66 % against a
#                    12.5 % break-even (a translation is ~12 us, a module
#                    ~96 us; W64_SPEC_N 0 vs 32: 127k extra translations
#                    remove 84k misses), but guessing the address after
#                    an unconditional transfer makes +89 % translations
#                    and removes no misses -- and after an *indirect*
#                    one it panics the EL71 firmware in 4.5 s.  tb_flush,
#                    ISA alignment and stale-TB-over-reprogrammed-flash
#                    all ruled out by experiment.  Ships off)
#   qemu b0152f3      (2026-09-16 round twenty-one, 0094: W64_BYTEPAD, the
#                    knob that prices emitted bytes against compile.
#                    0/40/120 -> 5.1/7.7/14.5 KB per module ->
#                    96/110/125 us: ~80 us fixed + 3.2 us/KB, so bytes
#                    are 17 % of the module cost and worth at most 2.2 %
#                    of wall driven to zero.  Closes the emitted-byte
#                    question three earlier rounds left ambiguous - their
#                    2.6-3.7 % cuts predicted 0.4 %, under every meter
#                    used.  Live-module count is flat 500->6000
#                    (tools/modgrow.mjs).  Module count is the only lever
#                    left on the 12.5 % pipeline.  Instrumentation only)
#   qemu 8d26a08      (2026-09-16 round twenty-one, 0093: W64_LOCALPAD, the
#                    knob that prices the ~70 locals every TB function
#                    declares.  wasm zeroes locals at entry and the
#                    baseline tier has no liveness analysis: +38.9 ns on a
#                    49.9 ns call there, free in the optimizing tier
#                    (tools/locals-probe.mjs).  The boot runs
#                    --liftoff-only +63 % and --wasm-tiering-budget=1000
#                    -3.1 %, so ~3.6 % of TB entries execute baseline code;
#                    in-app the spare locals are worth under 1 %.  Priced,
#                    not built.  Instrumentation only)
#   qemu 45968bc      (2026-09-16 round twenty-one, 0092: a lookup miss costs
#                    a wasm module - closeN == specMiss, and the pipeline is
#                    12.5 % of an EL71 boot at 74 us fixed + 1.25 us/KB.
#                    Speculation is edge-limited, not budget-limited
#                    (W64_SPEC_N 8 == 128), and the call-return address is
#                    the one statically-known indirect edge nothing
#                    recorded on the Thumb-1 split BL and blx <reg> paths -
#                    this core is an ARM926EJ-S, so that is every Thumb
#                    call.  Misses -2.6 %, 3/3 on fixed guest work, ~0.3 %
#                    of wall.  W64_NORETSPEC=1 A/Bs it)
#   qemu bd9ae89      (2026-09-16 round twenty-one, 0091: every TB entry is a
#                    return_call_indirect through the shared chain table
#                    (~11 M/s) and used to follow the chain slot into the
#                    *target's* descriptor for fidx and tidx - one cache
#                    line per TB in ~20 MB the execution path never
#                    otherwise reads, on the dependency chain of an
#                    indirect branch.  tools/dispatch-probe.mjs priced that
#                    load at +2.1 ns over a 256-TB working set and
#                    +8..9 ns over 1024-4096; the helpers and
#                    tb_set_jmp_target now carry the table index itself,
#                    tagged in the high half.  Boot window, fixed guest
#                    work, 3 interleaved pairs: el71 -5.4 %, s75 -4.5 %,
#                    cx70 -7.0 %, ke800 -10.8 %, 3/3 each)
#   qemu bad630a3    (2026-09-16 round twenty, 0090: the wasm64 op-suite leg
#                    had been a KNOWN HOLE since 2026-09-13 - "null function"
#                    in an Asyncify rewind on the versatilepb suite target,
#                    so the shipping backend had no op-suite coverage and
#                    lockstep+bootcheck carried it.  A no-onlylist build ran
#                    the suite green, and QEMU_COSTACK=1 on the suite target
#                    (tools/costack-suite.mjs) captured the 7 switch stacks;
#                    resolving them found 20 functions missing from
#                    configs/meson/asyncify-only.txt, all on the versatilepb
#                    machine-init / legacy-SCSI / board-reset path no phone
#                    boot reaches (versatile_init, vpb_init, scsi_*,
#                    resettable_*, sd_*, pl181_reset, ...).  Added as the
#                    minimal explicit set: wasm +12.6 KB, el71 boot A/B a tie
#                    (medians 29.45 s both legs, 3 interleaved pairs).
#                    WASM64_SUITE=1 run-tcg-isa.sh green 1156/1156 with
#                    serial byte-identical to native JIT; the KNOWN-HOLE
#                    special case in scripts/run-tcg-isa.sh is deleted so the
#                    leg gates again.  See doc/performance-handoff.md)
#   qemu b68326e2ab (2026-09-16 "icount: the post-window rt cap is a
#                    500 ms budget, not strict (0089)" - the wasm
#                    default becomes QEMU_ICOUNT_RTCAP=budget:30:500:
#                    banked for the guest's first 30 s of its own clock
#                    as before, then a bank capped at 500 ms instead of
#                    strict, so a post-boot stall is repaid by at most
#                    half a second of sprinted clock (strict left the
#                    phone behind wall forever, banked sprinted the
#                    whole stall: v 4.2 -> 38 s in 4 s of wall on a
#                    12x-throttled host).  Page ?rt= gains
#                    budget[:<win>[:<ms>]]; icount_rtcap_mode() gains
#                    3 = budget for the pill/diagnostics)
#   qemu d8643d756d (2026-09-15 "pmb887x: run DIF v1 transfers where they
#                    are asked for, not from a timer (0079)" - round
#                    fifteen, 0078-0079, is the SGOLD round.  The user
#                    added a CX70 fullflash and reported SGOLD as
#                    terrible; every earlier round measured SGOLD2
#                    (S75/EL71) or the LG, and hw/arm/pmb887x/dif_v1.c
#                    had never had the work dif_v2.c got.  The CX70
#                    never halts (64 halts/s against the S75's 27091)
#                    and burned ~116 MIPS to hold 1.07x real time, with
#                    ~17 % of its vCPU in the DIF/DMAC/SSI chain.  0078
#                    gives v1 the two things v2 already had: dif_mux()
#                    as a byte-lane table instead of a per-bit loop with
#                    two divisions and two modulos per bit per word, and
#                    a level filter on the two DMA request lines that
#                    were driven ~4x per word although the level almost
#                    never changes.  0079 stops handing every single
#                    word to timer_mod(transfer_timer, 0) - the deadline
#                    is zero, so the timer bought nothing, and because
#                    the DIF's breq then reached the DMAC from outside
#                    its run loop the DMAC armed its timer per burst too
#                    (10.87M against 10.86M bursts, where an S75 does
#                    99/s against 11660/s).  v2 has never called
#                    timer_mod at all.  CX70 wall time for a fixed 8G
#                    guest instructions: +6.2 % then +7.3 %, every step
#                    winning every interleaved pair; the display output
#                    is byte identical at a fixed instruction count.
#                    The meter is new - tools/workbench.mjs, because the
#                    SGOLD idle screen animates and idle MIPS swings
#                    ~15 % between runs of one build.  See
#                    doc/performance-handoff.md)
#   qemu b7822c674b (2026-09-15 two merges.  alula/dsp-stuff came in
#                    resolved to ours and changed no file: its two content
#                    commits are already here verbatim (the AFE cherry-pick
#                    has the same patch-id as alula's, and debfa3d6f5 is his
#                    RTC-seed commit), and what was left on his side is a
#                    dsp_hexdump() that indexes words where the offset
#                    counts bytes, the pre-fix dsp_realize(), and two #if 0
#                    debug blocks.  The merge is recorded so the branch
#                    stops looking behind.
#                    The one that matters is qemu-pmb887x master, plus a
#                    whitespace fix on top.  Master
#                    brought one commit that counts here, "pmb887x: Fix
#                    dsp crash on Siemens SGOLD phones" (PR #4): it lands
#                    inside dsp.c's #ifdef STUB_DSP block, which upstream
#                    leaves disabled and this branch enables, so it is
#                    inert there and live here.  dsp_realize() now seeds
#                    DSP shared-RAM word 0 from rom_version - the value
#                    board/dsp.c actually sets from the board's
#                    dsp.ram0_value - instead of the ram0_value *property*,
#                    which nothing ever sets and whose default is 0x0801.
#                    The boards that ask for 0x0602, siemens-cx75 and
#                    include/siemens-sgl-r65, get their own value for the
#                    first time; every other board already wanted 0x0801,
#                    which is why this never showed up on s75/el71/c81)
#   qemu cd0ecd359d (2026-09-15 "wasm-diag: gate the counters that ended
#                    up on hot paths (0076)" - the tip of the series.
#                    Round fourteen, 0074-0076, is about what it costs to
#                    stay unwindable.  -sASYNCIFY_ONLY names every
#                    function Asyncify must instrument for a coroutine
#                    switch, and instrumenting one is not free; the list
#                    still held eighteen entries reachable only from the
#                    vCPU thread, where rr_cpu_thread_fn() forbids
#                    coroutines outright and qemu_coroutine_switch()
#                    aborts, because the JIT'd TB frame below a helper
#                    cannot be instrumented at all.  They were the stack
#                    of a vCPU flash write, the case flash-blk.c had
#                    already moved to a main-loop bottom half - the fix
#                    deleted the stack, nothing deleted the entries.
#                    0074 removes them (verified with QEMU_COSTACK=1 and
#                    the new tools/asyncify-audit.mjs: 67 distinct frames
#                    on a 120 s S75 boot, all still covered, none of the
#                    eighteen among them).  0075 folds the MMIO fast path
#                    into its callers with a compile-time size, which
#                    also collapses a double bswap that cancelled on
#                    every little-endian device read.  0076 gates the
#                    ~14M diagnostic read-modify-writes a second that had
#                    accumulated on the hot paths (rebuild with
#                    WASM_DIAG_HOT_COUNTERS to get the rates back, and
#                    never wall-clock A/B such a build).  S75 idle vs
#                    round thirteen: +8.5 % MIPS, +8.4 % v/wall (4/4
#                    pairs); EL71 +3.4 % / +3.1 % (3/3) - less, because
#                    EL71 is compute-bound and spends little time in the
#                    functions this round un-instrumented.  See
#                    doc/performance-handoff.md)
#   qemu 4368d0317b (2026-09-15 "system/cpus: do not give the BQL back
#                    after every device access (0073)" - round thirteen,
#                    0070-0073, is mostly one
#                    finding: wasm has no relaxed atomic and no acquire
#                    fence, so every qatomic_set is a seq_cst
#                    iN.atomic.store and every smp_rmb() an atomic.fence,
#                    and QEMU pays for ordering it does not need here.
#                    0070 read the virtual clock without the seqlock's
#                    fence pair or the atomic publish (icount_get
#                    4.3 % -> 0.6 % of the vCPU; a ceiling probe said
#                    +5.0 % was available and the patch took +5.2 %),
#                    0072 did the same for cpu_handle_interrupt's
#                    per-pass qatomic_set_mb plus three other fixed
#                    costs, and 0073 stopped handing the BQL back after
#                    every one of the S75's three million device
#                    accesses a second (bounded by the rr loop's own
#                    unlock before every tcg_cpu_exec, so a missed
#                    release is one icount slice of delay, not a
#                    deadlock).  0071 is the other kind: a short hflags
#                    rebuild for a CPU with no M/AArch64/EL2/EL3/PMSA/v6,
#                    verified by a build that takes the short path,
#                    computes the generic answer anyway and counts
#                    disagreements - ~59M rebuilds, zero.  S75 idle vs
#                    round twelve: +14.6 % MIPS, +14.1 % v/wall (4/4
#                    pairs); EL71 +10.0 % / +9.9 % (3/3); KE800 boot
#                    -10 %.  See
#                    doc/performance-handoff.md)
#   qemu 9146059     (2026-09-20: merge pass over the two upstream topic
#                    branches that had grown off the wasm-browser-port tip.
#                    c2cb8c0 (origin/sgold-regression-fix, = smh mirror):
#                    the A32 TST/TEQ/CMP/CMN decode pinned the Rd nibble
#                    to 0000 and sent every other encoding to
#                    unallocated, but pre-V7 cores (the ARM926 in the
#                    SGOLD boards) decode the op, update the flags and
#                    skip the writeback — patched fullflashes branch into
#                    such literal-pool words.  9cb5eac
#                    (origin/pmb887x-usart-rx-pacing):
#                    usart_schedule_accept_input() waited one frame time
#                    and then let the chardev fill the whole RX FIFO, so a
#                    bulk transfer into the phone arrived as an interrupt
#                    storm — GSM L1 misses its deadline and the firmware
#                    resets; now paced to the baud rate and the FIFO fill
#                    level, one byte per frame.  Both merged clean on top
#                    of 474bff9 (the device-model review pass, which had
#                    itself been pushed to origin/wasm-browser-port and
#                    smh/wasm-browser-port); the pin also catches up to
#                    the gitlink, which 18bcbe5 had moved to 474bff9
#                    without bumping QEMU_PMB887X_REV from fada259)
#   bsp  63b56fa    (2026-09-22: master up to the commit before the pad
#                    rename.  ebbb9c5 + 4da7eb0 "update ke970 config" give
#                    the KE970 jog dial a [peripheral.SCROLL] of type
#                    ke970-scroll, bound to NAV_UP/NAV_DOWN through the new
#                    `key = {}` block, so the wheel the page now draws as a
#                    pad (site/keyboards.js, board ke970) reaches the
#                    firmware; the board also moves M_0/M_9 onto `voltage`
#                    and remaps USB_CHG_EN/TF_DAT1.  Deliberately NOT the
#                    tip: eb4f9fc "lib: fix pad names" swaps the names of
#                    GPIO 39 and 99 (DIF_HD <-> MMCI_DAT1) and names 32/33
#                    I2S2_RX/I2S2_TX, and rewrites five board configs
#                    against them, while qemu's own pad table — generated
#                    from bsp and committed in the submodule, gen/cpu_regs.h
#                    — still has the old names.  Those configs would bind
#                    the e71/el71/m72/s68 aliases to the wrong pin and fail
#                    the KE970 on an I2S2_RX the table does not have)
#   bsp  e79169f    (2026-09-20: master.  PR#6 landed upstream as e8d490e,
#                    so PMB887X_BSP_FIX_REV and its merge in
#                    scripts/sync-bsp.sh are gone — plain master is the
#                    whole checkout again.  New boards this round: M65,
#                    M75, ME75, SK65 and SL65 (4a18db7, 828613d,
#                    6b2db60), CX75 reworked onto the new
#                    siemens-sgl-sirius include, WHBABCON/WHBABSTAT bits
#                    (c19c914) and USART.cfg RX pacing.  The device list
#                    (site/fullflashes.js DEVICE_RULES) grew the matching
#                    filename rules; boards.tar is re-packed from this
#                    rev by scripts/pack-boards.sh)
#   bsp  23fc945    (2026-09-17: master + PR#6.  Master's 55752c5 comments
#                    out the [peripheral.RF] hd155153np entries on the x75
#                    and ELKA includes — the device the emulator has no
#                    table entry for, which used to abort board init with
#                    'hardware error: Unknown device: hd155153np' and which
#                    this project carried a bsp-patches/ workaround for
#                    until now — and maps CAMERA and PLAY_PAUSE into the
#                    S75 keypad matrix, so the two side keys the page
#                    already draws finally reach the firmware.  c0cf69a
#                    adds the END_CALL mapping to the SGOLD keypad,
#                    23fc945 the WHBABCON SETABEN/CLRABEN flags.  PR#6
#                    (e6e73d1, "LG boards read the RTC counter as packed
#                    calendar fields") is still unmerged upstream:
#                    PMB887X_BSP_FIX_REV keeps it merged onto the master
#                    pin — scripts/sync-bsp.sh does the merge, so a fresh
#                    clone reproduces it — because the [rtc]
#                    format = "calendar" it carries is what the LG firmware
#                    needs and the companion of qemu's RTC cnt-format
#                    commit)
export QEMU_PMB887X_REPO="https://github.com/Azq2/qemu-pmb887x"
#   qemu 2c59e1b    (2026-09-15 round eighteen, 0085: cpsr_write rebuilt hflags
#                    whenever the write mask covered M/E/IL -- true of every
#                    `msr cpsr_c` used to mask interrupts.  Require the bits
#                    to actually move: 873k/s -> 317k/s.  cx70 +1.0 % 3/3,
#                    el71 a tie.  Checked with --enable-debug-tcg.)
#   qemu f902fa1    (2026-09-15 round eighteen, 0084: TB_JMP_CACHE_BITS 12 -> 14.
#                    95 % of the qht fall-throughs were jump-cache conflict
#                    misses; 16k entries cut qht lookups per Mi by 62 % (el71)
#                    and 55 % (cx70).  Warm window: cx70 +3.5 %, el71 +1.6 %.
#                    16 bits is worse than 14 on both boards.  Affordable only
#                    because 0083 took tcg_flush_jmp_cache to ~0/s.)
#   qemu c07259d    (2026-09-16 round nineteen, 0088: 0086's split left ~14.5
#                    us per module outside every timer.  MOD_PRE_NS finds it in
#                    w64_batch_instantiate's prologue -- a DataView and a
#                    Float64Array rebuilt over the 2 GB shared buffer per call,
#                    and the module bytes copied twice because HEAPU8.slice()
#                    already returns a Uint8Array.  Views cached on buffer
#                    identity, wrapper dropped: 9.8 -> 5.3 us a module, 0.156 s
#                    per 25 s EL71 boot, ~0.62 % of wall.)
#   qemu 48926e6    (2026-09-16 round nineteen, 0087: the module-GC nudge --
#                    32 MB of garbage every 256 instantiations, ~170 MB/s at
#                    boot rates -- exists for SpiderMonkey, where module code
#                    is not GC pressure and the worker never yields.  V8 needs
#                    none of it and paid 0.27 s per 25 s EL71 boot, ~1.1 %.
#                    Decided from the user agent now; W64_GCNUDGE forces it.
#                    It does NOT inflate compile time: 2.8754 vs 2.8761 s.)
#   qemu 892eee3    (2026-09-16 round nineteen, 0086: instrumentation.  The
#                    topology commit split -- topoFull is 170 per 25 s EL71
#                    boot against topoVar 16872, and 122 of those are startup
#                    add_subregion, so 0083 left nothing there.  And MOD_NS
#                    decomposed: of 3.66 s, WebAssembly.Module is 2.82 and the
#                    import object 0.026.  Two sources differing 178x in count
#                    and 1.05x in bytes solve to ~80 us FIXED per module call;
#                    W64_SPEC_N 8/32/128 agrees at 83.6/82.7/83.3 us.  And
#                    W64_MODBENCH compiles one real module's bytes 200x back
#                    to back inside the vCPU worker: 12-31 us, against 83 us
#                    once in the normal flow -- four fifths of "compile" is
#                    cold cache, paid per compile event.)
#   qemu 1326888    (2026-09-15 round eighteen, 0083: a readonly flip is a
#                    flat-view *variant*, not a topology change.  The pmb887x
#                    EBU opens the NOR flash chip-select for a CFI command and
#                    closes it again -- 599 times a second on an EL71 boot --
#                    and each flip used to bump topo_gen, re-render every
#                    view and drop the romd stash.  Folding the readonly set
#                    into the stash signature makes the A-B-A toggle a hit.
#                    Boot window, fixed guest work: el71 +55.6 %, cx70
#                    +31.5 %, s75 +15.1 %.)
#   qemu d857b65    (2026-09-15 round seventeen, 0082: phase timers, and the
#                    deletion of the ldr pc,[pc,#-4] trampoline heuristic --
#                    guarded on !CF_PCREL since birth, so it had never run,
#                    and once woken it fires on one walked node in 35k.
#                    The timers replaced the discredited profiler for the
#                    one question it was still trusted on: the
#                    translate+compile pipeline is 19.5 % of EL71 boot wall,
#                    so the early boot is NOT compile-bound and tiering is
#                    capped at the ~10 % compile slice)
#   qemu 25c93a1    (2026-09-15 round sixteen, 0081: a key event arriving
#                    before wasm_display_init() created its bottom half took
#                    the whole module down with "memory access out of bounds"
#                    -- qemu_bh_schedule(NULL)'s atomic lands on a valid wasm
#                    address and succeeds, so the fault surfaces three
#                    dereferences later.  It failed every board of bootcheck
#                    on every dist while looking like host memory pressure.
#                    Regression test: tools/earlykey.mjs)
#   qemu b2fa7e8    (2026-09-15 round sixteen, 0080: wasm-diag counters that
#                    price speculation, the jump cache and arm_rebuild_hflags.
#                    Instrumentation only, all of it wasm-gated.  They exist
#                    because this round found that the wasm profile's
#                    per-function self-time names the wrong function -- see
#                    doc/lessons.md "The wasm profile names the wrong
#                    function")
export QEMU_PMB887X_REV="686c6d67ce"
export QEMU_PMB887X_BRANCH="wasm-browser-port"

export PMB887X_BSP_REPO="https://github.com/Azq2/pmb887x-dev"
# bsp master (2026-09-20: e79169f).  PR#6 ("LG boards read the RTC counter
# as packed calendar fields") landed upstream as e8d490e, so the separate
# PMB887X_BSP_FIX_REV pin is gone — plain master is the whole checkout again.
# That round added the boards the device list now ships: M65, M75, ME75, SK65
# and SL65 (4a18db7, 828613d, 6b2db60), reworked CX75 onto the new
# siemens-sgl-sirius include, added the WHBABCON/WHBABSTAT bits (c19c914) and
# paced USART RX in modules/USART.cfg.
#
# 2026-09-22 → 63b56fa, for ebbb9c5 + 4da7eb0 "update ke970 config": the
# KE970's jog dial is a board peripheral at last —
#   [peripheral.SCROLL] type = "ke970-scroll"
#   out = { A = "SCROLL_KEY_A", B = "SCROLL_KEY_B" }
#   key = { CW = "NAV_UP", CCW = "NAV_DOWN" }
# — so UP/DOWN reach the firmware as wheel detents instead of falling off
# the end of a keymap that never had them (the two [keyboard.gpio] lines
# were commented out before).  It also moves the battery/current channels
# onto `voltage` and remaps USB_CHG_EN/TF_DAT1.
#
# NOT master's tip: eb4f9fc "lib: fix pad names" swaps the names of GPIO 39
# and 99 (DIF_HD ↔ MMCI_DAT1) and names 32/33 I2S2_RX/I2S2_TX, then rewrites
# five board configs against the new names.  qemu's pad table is generated
# from bsp and committed in the submodule (hw/arm/pmb887x/gen/cpu_regs.h,
# still DIF_HD=39 / MMCI_DAT1=99), so those configs would bind e71/el71/m72/
# s68 aliases to the wrong pin and fail the KE970 outright on I2S2_RX, which
# that table does not have.  It can be picked up once qemu regenerates.
export PMB887X_BSP_REV="63b56fa5701b0b9f33a28de1446764a30afbdf9e"

# Toolchain versions (mirror qemu's tests/docker/dockerfiles/emsdk-wasm64-cross.docker)
export EMSDK_VERSION="4.0.10"
export ZLIB_VERSION="1.3.2"
export PIXMAN_VERSION="0.44.2"
export LIBFFI_VERSION="v3.5.2"
export GLIB_VERSION="2.84.0"
